WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.510
- So both of you are
mothers of black sons.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.090
So what kind of conversations
are taking place

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.660
in your homes right now?

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.050
- Ah well, I mean it's
a normal conversation,

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.320
a conversation that's
of course been going on

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.940
since the beginning of time

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.570
and even from the days
that we first got here.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.453
It's a conversation of being careful.

00:00:20.338 --> 00:00:22.890
That when you go out, be kind.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.280
You think about in 1800's, you know,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.040
don't make eye contact.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.150
There's always the conversation.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.040
Every generation it's different.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.140
Now, and we say, you know,
be nice to the police.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.780
My brothers were told, say "yes sir."

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:37.830
But now it doesn't matter.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:41.580
Now it's just, be
careful, do what you can.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.440
Have all your ducks in a row.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.200
The conversations very,
my son is nine years old

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.690
so I was hoping that this conversation

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.380
wouldn't be around any more.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.260
But every year I see that I
have to have the conversation

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.520
and my son said to me
when I told him about

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.577
what happened to George Floyd, he said

00:00:57.577 --> 00:00:59.250
"Am I gonna die one day?"

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.829
- Oh my gosh!

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:02.797
- It's a hard thing being you know-

00:01:02.797 --> 00:01:05.850
- It really, no, no no, go ahead.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.520
I was just gonna say, but
I just wanna say this,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.010
what I want to say to people is,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.100
my son is like your son.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.830
Our children are all the same.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.440
We're mothers.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.770
They grow up to be men but
don't be afraid of my son.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.290
Don't be afraid of me.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.590
We are people, we are all people

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.120
and we are all just trying
to raise our families

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.540
and have good lives.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.418
Go ahead Sherry, say what you gotta say.

00:01:30.418 --> 00:01:31.410
- It's the most ridiculous
thing I think though

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.920
that you have to say that.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.960
- Kelly it's really, it's just
like Kim said, to piggy-back.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.040
It's awful that we're still
experiencing the same thing

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.810
that people went through
in the 50's and the 60's

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.990
when they were marching.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.490
And to have to tell our sons,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.060
we're black mothers so our
prayer is very different,

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.130
to tell our sons how to
survive just to make it home

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.940
is very anguishing and it's devastating.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.420
And just like Kim, my son likes the WWE,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.810
he watches Nickelodeon, he's
got a great sense of humor

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.460
and he just wants a girlfriend

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.470
and that's just like anybody else's son.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.262
But to know that somebody
won't get to the core

00:02:05.262 --> 00:02:08.460
of how beautiful he is
because of his skin color

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.070
is really frightening to me.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.020
And, you know, we just call for people to

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.260
speak out against racism

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.210
and speak out when you
see something wrong.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.590
Speak out.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.360
And also, there's things
in the rooms that you hear

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.330
that Kim and I won't hear
because we're two black women

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.200
and we just ask for people
when you say, what can we do?

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.570
Speak out and say, where
are the people of color

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.010
behind the scenes that
can make a difference

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.590
on even TV

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.990
so that our sons can grow up
and be and do what they love

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.650
like everybody else's children.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.580
- No, and it's the same thing I think with

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.110
even police brutality.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.360
It's like, hey, okay, there
are great cops out there

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.260
but you're not great if
you're just standing by.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.600
Like, you need to say something.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.630
Like you have to call people
out and, it's gotta change.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.270
And so anyway, that's why I love,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.520
I know it's not uncomfortable for me

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.290
but I know it can be
uncomfortable for some people,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.170
especially in the spotlight,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.010
but I really appreciate
you being very honest

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.360
and vulnerable and truthful

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.370
because I think that's the only way

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.080
people are gonna hear you,
you know, whoever you are

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.250
wanting to get your message out.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.400
So, anyway, thank you for
discussing that with me.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.550
I really appreciate it.

